# OpenOrc local development environment (draft)
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# - `.env` is git-ignored and must never be committed.
# - devserver.sh loads `.env`; variables already exported in the shell win.
# - Never put real project refs, tokens, or credentials in tracked files.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase (ephemeral dev branches)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Ref of the parent hosted Supabase project from which ephemeral dev branches
# are created. Required by devserver.sh when Supabase automation is enabled
# (the default). Take this value from your local configuration.
OPENORC_SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=

# Optional safety guard.
# When set, devserver.sh refuses branch operations against this project ref.
OPENORC_SUPABASE_PRODUCTION_PROJECT_REF=

# Supabase CLI access token used for branch create/delete against the hosted
# parent project. Obtain from the Supabase dashboard; treat as a secret.
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local ports (optional)
#
# devserver.sh defaults: app 8081, API 3000. Uncomment to override.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#LOCAL_APP_PORT=8081
#LOCAL_API_PORT=3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenOrc process configuration (optional)
#
# OPENORC_ENV labels the process environment (default development).
# OPENORC_EXECUTION_MODE selects where logical operations are delivered
# (default regular):
#   - regular (default): the API process executes operations through its
#     bounded local executor. No RQ worker and no queue backend are
#     required, started, or contacted. Capacity is bounded per serving
#     process through OPENORC_LOCAL_EXECUTOR_WORKERS (default 2, minimum 1)
#     and OPENORC_LOCAL_EXECUTOR_PENDING (default 8, minimum 1).
#   - queue (opt-in): operations are delivered through RQ/Valkey to
#     independently deployed workers; requires an explicitly supplied
#     VALKEY_URL (see the Queue backend section) and fails configuration
#     without one.
# API process: OPENORC_API_HOST / OPENORC_API_PORT (default 127.0.0.1:3000).
# OPENORC_API_RELOAD=1 enables ASGI reload for local development.
# API/worker processes read DATABASE_URL for direct Postgres access; in
# devserver runs it is exported per-run from the ephemeral branch (see
# Runtime-generated values below). Pool bounds are per process, never
# deployment-wide: OPENORC_DB_POOL_MIN / OPENORC_DB_POOL_MAX (defaults 1/10)
# and OPENORC_DB_POOL_TIMEOUT seconds (default 30).
#
# The API serving process additionally owns one process-local PostgreSQL
# notification listener (issue #397). OPENORC_DB_LISTENER_URL is the
# optional dedicated listener DSN; when unset the listener falls back to
# DATABASE_URL. LISTEN is session-bound: a known Supabase transaction-pooler
# port (6543) is refused at serving startup, and an explicitly supplied DSN
# must carry the same database name as DATABASE_URL (the operator invariant:
# both endpoints target the same PostgreSQL deployment and database). The
# listener contributes exactly one extra database connection per serving
# process. The DSN is credential material and is never logged.
#OPENORC_DB_LISTENER_URL=
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#OPENORC_ENV=development
#OPENORC_EXECUTION_MODE=regular
#OPENORC_LOCAL_EXECUTOR_WORKERS=2
#OPENORC_LOCAL_EXECUTOR_PENDING=8
# The existing runtime-side neutral non-repository directory the Reviewer
# session is constructed in (issue #253). Required when Task-workflow
# operations run (the planning bootstrap establishes the Reviewer there).
#OPENORC_REVIEWER_NEUTRAL_DIRECTORY=/srv/neutral/review
#OPENORC_API_HOST=127.0.0.1
#OPENORC_API_PORT=3000
#OPENORC_API_RELOAD=1
# Optional browser-CORS origin allow-list (issue #369): comma-separated
# absolute http(s) origins granted CORS headers by the API process. Unset or
# blank keeps CORS disabled (the default in every environment); wildcards,
# path-bearing values, and malformed entries fail configuration. HTTP
# mechanism only — never application authorization policy; leave unset when
# an edge proxy already owns CORS.
#OPENORC_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication verification (Supabase Auth)
#
# API/worker authentication verifies Supabase Auth access tokens against the
# project's asymmetric signing keys (JWKS). It consumes the Supabase project
# URL (SUPABASE_URL) to derive the Auth issuer/JWKS source and the expected
# authenticated audience:
#
#   OPENORC_SUPABASE_JWT_AUDIENCE  default "authenticated"
#
# SUPABASE_URL is runtime-generated in devserver runs and must never be added
# to `.env` (see Runtime-generated values below). Production deployments must
# supply it: OPENORC_ENV=production fails startup when SUPABASE_URL is
# missing or malformed. v1 is GitHub-only sign-in: configure Supabase Auth
# with GitHub as the only enabled user sign-in provider.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#OPENORC_SUPABASE_JWT_AUDIENCE=authenticated

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub integration (OpenOrc GitHub App)
#
# Deployment/bootstrap credentials for the deployed OpenOrc GitHub App.
# Optional on this shared environment surface: each requirement is enforced
# by the component that consumes it, which fails fast when absent, so
# unrelated processes never need it. Secret material below is never
# Workspace data, never stored in `openorc.*` tables or Supabase Vault,
# never returned through ordinary APIs, and never logged or attached to
# telemetry. A supplied-but-blank value of any of these fails closed
# without echoing the value.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The App's numeric App ID (identity material).
#OPENORC_GITHUB_APP_ID=

# The App's PEM private key (secret material; supplied verbatim, never
# stripped or normalized).
#OPENORC_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=

# The App's webhook secret, used to verify inbound delivery signatures at
# POST /api/github/webhooks (secret material).
#OPENORC_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The App's user-flow client ID (identity material) for the Profile-scoped
# GitHub App user authorization that represents the accountable Owner for
# engineering-record GitHub writes.
#OPENORC_GITHUB_APP_USER_FLOW_CLIENT_ID=

# The App's user-flow client secret (secret material).
#OPENORC_GITHUB_APP_USER_FLOW_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Queue backend (queue execution mode only; explicitly supplied)
#
# VALKEY_URL selects the Redis-compatible queue backend used only when
# OPENORC_EXECUTION_MODE=queue. It is never implied: no localhost default is
# selected, and queue-mode configuration/startup fails without an explicitly
# supplied value. Regular mode requires no queue backend at all. Supply the
# actual endpoint in your private .env or the deployment environment; this
# tracked template intentionally contains no endpoint value.
#
# devserver.sh queue-mode lifecycle (only when queue mode is selected):
#   - a server already responding at the configured local URL is reused and
#     never stopped on exit, but the selected local DB is flushed first;
#   - a server started by devserver.sh runs the redis-server binary directly
#     (never brew services) with persistence disabled and is stopped on exit;
#   - non-local URLs are never flushed and never managed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#VALKEY_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Queue-mode detached-turn observation registration (queue execution mode only)
#
# The serving API/control-plane process hosts the ONE long-lived detached-turn
# observation host (issue #346); a queue dispatch worker must positively attest
# a live exact-session observation registration on its private authenticated
# handshake BEFORE admitting any detached model turn, and never enqueues when
# it cannot attest one. Both settings are required (fail fast) only in queue
# mode; regular mode hosts its observer in-process and requires neither.
#
#   OPENORC_QUEUE_OBSERVER_REGISTRATION_URL   the serving API base URL that
#                                             hosts the observer (http/https)
#   OPENORC_QUEUE_OBSERVER_REGISTRATION_SECRET the deployment-level shared
#                                             credential for the private
#                                             handshake (secret material;
#                                             never logged, never echoed)
#
# The handshake route is private/authenticated and nonpublic: network
# isolation owns keeping it unreachable from outside the deployment, and the
# v1 deployment assumption is ONE API instance per deployment (multiple
# instances must not silently split in-memory session registrations).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#OPENORC_QUEUE_OBSERVER_REGISTRATION_URL=
#OPENORC_QUEUE_OBSERVER_REGISTRATION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ngrok (optional)
#
# Reserved ngrok URL for webhook/callback testing.
# ngrok is skipped entirely when this is unset.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#NGROK_RESERVED_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime-generated values
#
# The following are produced per-run by devserver.sh from the ephemeral
# Supabase branch and exported to the process tree. Never add them here
# or to `.env`:
#
#   SUPABASE_URL (also consumed by the API/worker for Supabase Auth
#                JWT verification)
#   SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY
#   SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY
#   DATABASE_URL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Frontend (apps/app)
#
# The product SPA has its own Vite environment file: apps/app/.env.example
# (copy to apps/app/.env). Vite only exposes VITE_-prefixed variables to the
# client bundle; see apps/app/.env.example for the API base URL contract.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------